<h2>How the decree came about</h2>
<p>The husband petitioned for divorce under Section 13 of the Hindu Marriage Act, alleging cruelty and desertion. The wife never appeared; ex parte proceedings were drawn, and an ex parte decree of divorce followed in January 2021. Her account was simple: she did not know. The summons had been served on her father — but she was not living with her parents at the relevant time; she was with her mother at her maternal grandparents&rsquo; house. In March 2022 she applied under Order IX Rule 13 CPC to set aside the decree, citing lack of knowledge and the pandemic. The Family Court rejected her application in July 2024, and she appealed.</p>

<h2>The Division Bench&rsquo;s reasoning</h2>
<p>The court&rsquo;s analysis begins and ends with service. &ldquo;It has remained undisputed that the service of summons was not effected personally upon the appellant&rdquo; (para 8). Since the wife was living apart from her parents, service on the father was not sufficient to bind her (para 9); and the COVID-19 period supplied a further explanation for the time taken to apply (para 10). The operative direction: &ldquo;The learned Family Court shall proceed afresh in the matter by granting an opportunity to the appellant to defend herself&rdquo; (para 11).</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>The remarriage complication:</strong> the husband resisted the application by pointing to his second marriage, evidenced by an Arya Samaj certificate. That could not cure the service defect — and the certificate itself is under challenge in the wife&rsquo;s pending Section 11 HMA petition and a Section 156(3) CrPC proceeding. A spouse who remarries on the strength of an ex parte decree, while the period for challenging it is alive, assumes the risk that the decree — and with it the lawfulness of the remarriage — may unravel.</p>
</div>

<h2>Service in matrimonial cases: why the rules bite harder</h2>
<p>Order V CPC requires service on the party personally, or on an authorised agent; service on an adult family member is a fallback hedged with conditions, and a parent at a different address is not a substitute for the party herself. Matrimonial litigation sharpens the point: husband and wife in conflict often know exactly where the other resides, and summons routed to the natal family home of an estranged wife — a address she may have left — invites precisely this result. Family Courts, for their part, are expected to scrutinise service reports before proceeding ex parte in a case that will dissolve a marriage; the consequences of a false step are measured in remarriages, children and years.</p>

<h2>Practical guidance</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li>Petitioners: invest in service. Personal service, speed-post to the actual residence with tracking, and where permitted, court-sanctioned substituted service after genuine attempts — shortcuts create decrees that cannot be safely acted upon.</li>
  <li>Do not remarry on a fresh ex parte decree without legal advice on the limitation and knowledge rules; the apparent finality can be illusory.</li>
  <li>Respondents who discover an ex parte decree: move immediately under Order IX Rule 13, plead the date and mode of knowledge with precision, and support the actual-residence case with documents (ration card, Aadhaar address, school records, electoral rolls).</li>
  <li>Pandemic-period delays continue to receive sympathetic treatment where they genuinely explain the gap — but they supplement, not replace, a sound explanation.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<p>The appeal restores the matter to where it should have begun: a contested hearing, both parties present, on the merits of the marriage&rsquo;s alleged breakdown. Whatever the eventual outcome, it will now rest on evidence tested in the presence of both spouses — which is what a decree changing personal status requires.</p>
